course: chemistry
lesson: 23
title: Browning apples, rotting and mould
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cut an apple for your lunchbox and by break time the white flesh has gone brown. Cutting opened the apple's cells, so air reached their chemicals, and an enzyme helped them react with oxygen. A squeeze of lemon slows it, because the acid stops the enzyme working well.
explain: Food goes off in two main ways: its own chemistry changes, like a banana ripening and browning, and tiny living things move in: bacteria and fluffy mould growing from spores in the air.
explain: Germs need water and warmth. So people keep food by taking those away: the fridge slows them, drying makes raisins, lots of sugar makes jam, and salt kept fish and ham for months long before fridges existed.
explain: Rotting is not only bad news. In a compost heap, microbes break down peelings and leaves and give out so much heat that a big heap can steam on a frosty morning.
fact: A cut apple goes brown because air reaches its chemicals and an enzyme helps them react with oxygen; lemon juice slows this because its acid stops the enzyme working well.
fact: Cut avocado browns by the same enzyme-and-oxygen reaction; covering it tightly helps, while leaving the stone in only protects the part it touches.
fact: As a banana ripens, starch turns into sugar and the skin browns; ripening is controlled by a gas called ethylene that fruit gives off, so fruit in a bowl ripens together, and hard fruit shut in a bag with a ripe banana ripens faster.
fact: Mould grows from tiny spores in the air and likes damp, warm, sugary food.
fact: Mould sends threads deep into soft food, so cutting off the visible spot does not make soft food like strawberries safe.
fact: Cold slows down bacteria, mould and chemical reactions, so food lasts longer in the fridge.
fact: A freezer stops germs growing almost completely but does not kill most of them, so defrosted meat must still be cooked properly.
fact: Drying removes the water germs need, so dried fruit keeps for months; ancient Egyptians dried food in the sun to store it.
fact: Lots of sugar pulls water out of germs and stops them growing, so jam keeps for months; low-sugar jam and opened jam still go mouldy.
fact: A "best before" date is about quality and a "use by" date is about safety; dry food after its best-before date is often fine, which helps stop waste.
fact: A compost heap gets warm because microbes break down the scraps and release energy as heat.
fact: Salt pulls water out of food so germs cannot grow; sailors and others salted meat and fish before fridges existed.
exercise: Why does a cut apple go brown? | Its chemicals react with oxygen, helped by an enzyme | It soaks up dirt and dust floating in the kitchen air | It is starting to grow mould already | Light from the window burns it
exercise: Why does lemon juice keep apple slices white? | Its acid stops the enzyme working well | It paints a yellow coat over them | It adds sugar that hides the brown | It freezes the apple's surface
exercise: Does leaving the stone in guacamole stop it browning? | Only where the stone touches it | Yes, the stone protects it all | Yes, the stone gives off lemon juice | No, the stone makes it browner
exercise: What happens inside a banana as it ripens? | Starch turns into sugar | Sugar turns into starch | Water turns into oil | Its seeds start to grow
exercise: Why does a bowl of fruit ripen together? | Fruit gives off a ripening gas, ethylene | The fruits slowly share their juice with each other | The bowl is warmer than the fridge | Flies carry ripeness between them
exercise: Where does fluffy mould on an orange come from? | Tiny spores in the air | Seeds inside the orange | Water from the tap | The orange's own peel
exercise: Is it safe to cut the mouldy part off a strawberry and eat the rest? | No, mould threads reach deep into soft food | Yes, mould only ever lives on the outside surface | Yes, if you wash it in cold water | No, because strawberries are always unsafe
exercise: Why does food last longer in the fridge? | Cold slows down germs and reactions | Cold kills every single germ at once | The fridge light keeps food fresh | The fridge adds salt to food
exercise: Why must frozen meat be cooked properly after defrosting? | Freezing does not kill most germs | Frozen meat goes poisonous in the freezer | Defrosting adds new germs to it | Cold meat cannot be digested
exercise: Why does dried fruit keep for months? | Germs cannot grow without water | Drying adds a coat of salt | The sun turns the fruit into sugar crystals | Drying makes the fruit too hard to bite
exercise: Why does jam keep for months? | Lots of sugar stops germs growing | Jam is always kept frozen until it is opened | Jam has vinegar in it | Fruit in jam is cooked to stone
exercise: Why does low-sugar jam need to be kept in the fridge? | Less sugar means germs can grow more easily | Low-sugar jam melts at room temperature | It is made with milk instead of fruit | It attracts more flies than other jam
exercise: What is the difference between "best before" and "use by"? | Best before is quality, use by is safety | Best before is safety, use by is quality | They mean exactly the same | Use by is only for frozen food
exercise: Why does a compost heap get warm? | Microbes breaking down scraps give off heat | Sunlight is trapped under the leaves and builds up | Worms rub together and make heat | The scraps catch fire inside
exercise: How did sailors keep meat on long voyages before fridges? | They salted it | They painted it | They kept it in fresh water | They wrapped it in paper
exercise: Why does salt keep fish from going off? | It pulls out the water germs need | It makes the fish too heavy to rot | It cooks the fish without heat | It freezes the fish slowly
test: Josh cuts apple slices for a picnic and squeezes lemon on them. His mate laughs. Who's right? | Josh: the acid slows the browning | The mate: lemon makes them browner | Neither: apples never go brown | The mate: lemon makes them rot
test: "bread's got a green spot, I'll just pick it off and eat the rest". Is that a good idea for soft food? | No, mould threads can go deep inside | Yes, mould is only on the surface | Yes, green mould is always safe | No, because bread can never be eaten
test: Mum wants the hard green avocado to ripen faster. What could she do? | Put it in a bag with a ripe banana | Put it in the freezer overnight to soften it | Soak it in salty water | Keep it in a dark cupboard alone
test: A yoghurt is two days past "use by" and the biscuits are a month past "best before". Which should you be more careful with? | The yoghurt | The biscuits | Both are equally fine | Both must be eaten at once
test: If you left a bowl of grapes and a box of raisins on the table for a month, what would you expect? | The grapes would rot, the raisins would keep | Both would rot at the same speed | The raisins would rot first | Both would stay perfectly fresh
test: "frozen chicken's been in the freezer ages so the germs are all dead, right?" | No, freezing mostly stops germs but doesn't kill them | Yes, freezing kills every germ | Yes, but only after a year | No, freezing makes germs grow faster and faster in the cold
test: On a frosty morning, Grandpa's compost heap is steaming. Ella thinks it's on fire. What's true? | Microbes breaking down the scraps make heat | It is on fire and needs water | Someone poured a kettle of hot water over it | The frost is boiling away
test: A pirate game says sailors kept meat fresh for months in barrels of sugar water. What did they really use? | Salt | Sugar water | Fresh river water | Ice from home
test: Why might a banana go brown faster in the fruit bowl next to other ripe fruit? | Ripe fruit gives off ethylene gas | Other fruit pushes the banana's skin | Bananas hate being near apples | The bowl keeps the banana too cold
test: "opened the jam months ago, it's still fine coz jam lasts forever" | Opened jam still goes mouldy eventually | Jam truly never goes off once it's made | Only strawberry jam goes off | Jam turns into honey over time
test: If you threw half a dried apricot and half a fresh apricot into a warm damp cupboard, which would grow mould first? | The fresh one, it has more water | The dried one, it has more sugar | Both at exactly the same time | Neither, apricots never grow mould
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cut apple turns brown upon exposure to air. | Its chemicals react with oxygen, assisted by an enzyme | It absorbs dust and pollutants from the atmosphere | Mould spores colonise it within minutes | Light causes the flesh to burn
exam: It is given that a foodstuff bears the label "use by". Select the statement most accurately characterising that label. | It pertains to safety, and the food may be unsafe thereafter | It pertains solely to flavour and appearance | It pertains to the date of manufacture | It is of no consequence for perishable goods
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of freezing upon germs in meat? | Growth is almost halted, but most germs are not killed | All germs are destroyed instantaneously | Germs multiply more rapidly at low temperature | Freezing renders meat safe without cooking
exam: Ascertain the principle by which salt, sugar and drying preserve food. | They deprive germs of the water they require | They raise the temperature of the food | They supply oxygen that destroys germs | They convert the food into a mineral
